# Grade your partners

Week 15 · due Monday 7 December, 11:59 p.m. · 100 points · Graded items

## Purpose (page note: three-box arrow chain, Builds on your earlier learning → This Learning Experience → Prepare you for)

**Builds on your earlier learning.** A classmate, or more than one, asked you to evaluate (grade) their Sprint 3 work and you agreed ([My quality assurance partner](my-grader.html)); they have sent you their [Sprint 3 report](my-sprint-3-report.html) and their quality assurance plan.

**This Learning Experience.** Learn to evaluate (grade) someone's work against terms they set for themselves, from the evidence in front of you, in a way they can learn from.

**Prepare you for.** Enter your scores here so that they become your classmate's grade for task 1 of their report, after Prof. Sathya reviews them.

## Look back

(Page note: previous item is My Sprint 3 report. When this browser holds cst349.s3.4.leave, show it under "At the end of your report you wrote:". Otherwise: "Open your My Sprint 3 report submission on Canvas and read your last sentence again.")

You are about to do for a classmate what your quality assurance partner is doing for you. Read what you wrote at the end of your own report first.

○ I have sent my report to my quality assurance partner
○ I have not sent it yet
○ My quality assurance partner has already scored me

**The standard I am holding.** One sentence: what you would want your quality assurance partner to do with a line of yours that has thin evidence, because that is what you will do with your classmate's.

## How this works

Read each classmate's report, then fill in one block below for that classmate. Score their task 1 against their own lines, line by line, from the evidence in the report. Then answer four questions about how you graded, so that Prof. Sathya can see your reasoning and your classmate can see what you saw.

Score from what is in the report. If the evidence for a line is not there, score the line as not met, even if you believe your classmate did the work, and write in your sentence what was missing, so that they know what to show next time.

## The form

(Page note: start with one block; "Add another classmate" adds a removable block. Preserve saved blocks and omit untouched blocks from the copied text.)

### Classmate 1 (page note: repeatable block)

**Classmate's name** (page note: input with the roster datalist)

**The lines of their plan and my scores.** Copy each of their lines with its points, give the points you award from the evidence in their report, and add one sentence that points at the evidence, or at where it should have been.

   *Placeholder:* Line 1 — their words · their points → my points · why ⏎ Line 2 — … ⏎ Line 3 — … ⏎ Line 4 — …

   **Total I award for task 1** (page note: a number box, 0 to 100)

**The grading itself.** Answer each question in a sentence or two.

1. **Were their lines checkable?** Say whether you could answer yes or no to each line from the report alone. Identify any line you could not check, and say what would have made it checkable.

2. **Where was the evidence strongest?** Identify the line and describe the evidence.

3. **Where did you have to make a judgment call?** Identify the line, say what the report showed, and say how you decided. If the judgment was straightforward, explain which evidence made it clear.

4. **What would you tell them?** Write one thing to keep doing and one thing to do differently, in the words you would use if they were sitting next to you.

(Page note: the copied text carries, per classmate, the name, the lines with scores, the total, and the four answers under their question headings.)

## Self-check

Tick each line your submission meets. Your ticks are copied with your answers; Prof. Sathya verifies each one.

- [ ] Every classmate who chose me has a block, with every line of their plan scored · 30 pts
- [ ] Each score has a sentence that points at evidence in the report, or at its absence · 30 pts
- [ ] The four questions are answered for each classmate · 25 pts
- [ ] My comment to each classmate includes one thing to keep and one thing to change · 15 pts

**What makes it strong.** Your classmate can read your block and see which sentence of their report earned or lost each point, and Prof. Sathya can read your judgment call and reach the same decision from your sentence alone.

**Grading.** 100 points, in Graded assignments, for how you evaluated, not for the scores you gave. Prof. Sathya reviews every block before a score reaches a classmate's grade and may adjust a score; your points for this item stay the same when he does.

